From e5ad82c7776a01850d1fbadefa848c7d46c37a76 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: djrobson5 <256682087+djrobson5@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 13:31:57 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 20/21] Metal: a barrier that does not concern the open render
 pass leaves it open

barriers() ended the active encoder on every barrier, so a barrier on
textures a big pass has nothing to do with (the game moves two 64x64 textures
in the middle of its scene pass) stored the whole multisampled colour and depth
and loaded them again in a second pass.

With MetalDevice::keepPassOnBarrier on, the pass stays open when it is a
render pass and nothing in the barrier can have been written by it: no buffer
(memexport writes buffers from vertex shaders), no attachment of the pass, no
texture placed in a pool (its memory may alias an attachment), no draw with a
pipeline layout that has a read-write texture range (the main layout has none,
so only attachments are written), no texture moved to a layout the pass could
write it in (general, unknown), and no full clear pending. The reason a
barrier ended the pass is counted (g_metalBarrierStats).

The ordering stays that of ending the pass at the barrier: the pass waits at
the barrier for the fences the barrier's source stages updated so far (the
draws after it read what earlier encoders wrote; Metal holds a render
encoder's fence waits for the encoder, which only orders it after work that is
already encoded), and the barrier is applied for the encoders after the pass
when the pass ends, so they wait for the fence the pass updates: a texture the
barrier moves to be written is not overwritten while the pass still samples it
through the bindless table. Applying the barrier at once would let them
overtake the pass. A fence is not waited for and updated by one encoder, so a
pass that waits for the graphics fence ends by updating the next one.

The barrier's textures are looked at through the common base class (a swap
chain drawable goes through barriers() too), and a draw without a pipeline
layout is tolerated.
